Let $p_i=\Pr(X=i)$ and $q_j=\Pr(Y=j)$ have finite supports. Independence gives $\Pr(X+Y=s)=\sum_i p_iq_{s-i}$. A distribution is uniform on its support if all its nonzero probabilities are equal. The proposed implication is that a uniform convolution forces both input distributions to be uniform on their respective supports. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09843}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Suppose $X, Y$ are finitely-supported independent random variables taking integer values such that $X + Y$ is uniformly distributed on its range. Are $X$ and $Y$ themselves uniformly distributed on their ranges? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

